detector 1 pipeline

ncounts - number of counts in the group

The list of MIRI procedures:
1. Data quality - generates dq map from reference files
2. Saturation check - selects pixels with ncounts above the saturation limit (plus (+1) neighbors pixels- optional)
Note: original pipeline probably does not added a 'saturation' flag to data quality map 
3. Reset anomaly - the latest reference file has zero correction - NO CORRECTION AT THIS STEP
Note: this correction should be applied in dark subtraction procedure, according to fits header of the reference file
4. First frame - set DQ=1 for the pixels in the first group (in the integration), i.e. 'DO NOT USE' (FLAG = 1)
5. Last frame  - set DQ=1 for the pixels in the last group
6. Linear correction - makes correction for nonlinearity of ncounts for each group - Y[i] is changed to polynom of Y[i]
7. RSCD - does not applied for the first integration - NO CORRECTION AT THIS STEP
8. Dark subtraction - subtracts dark carrurent frpm all groups - it is taken from reference files (~10% of ncounts value). 
Note: I did not find the RESET correction at this step
9. Reference pixel - subtracts signal from reference pixels (not illuminated) from all other pixels - Typical value??
10. Jump detection - detects spikes - chacnge of the slopes between groups in pixels, and select the groups as "CR" in DQ map (neighbors pixels are also selected as CR - optional)
11. Slope fitting - fits the signal in good pixels and derives the slope and its uncertainty

Comments:

10. Jump detection

There is a problem with the algorithm.
The procedure is iterative:
First iteration:
Calculate the difference in ncounts between groups: Y(i+1)-Y(i), it is called as "first diff" or slope,
Exclude pixel with highest slope
Calculate median slope in good (DQ=0) pixels.
If the absolute value of ("first diff" - median) > X sigma, then this group is selected as CR in DQ map.
Sigma is calculated as sqrt(first diff + read-error-noise**2), read-error~noise ~ 50 counts, it is usually higher than 'first diff'
Second iteration:
The "CR" group is excluded and the first step is repeated.
The main problem is the pixels with non-linear slope is selected as CR-pixels. 

The default value of X is 20. I use X=5. The distribution of "first diff - mean" is close to the normal distribution.

11. Slope fitting

The procedure fits only "good" groups in the pixels map - with DQ flag == 0
If the number of good pixels == 1, then slope = 0, and variance_slope = 1e9
If the number of good pixels == 2, then slope = signal(1) - signal(0), variance_slope = sqrt(2)*read_noise
If the number of good pixels > 2   then slope is calculated using 'optimal weights method'
-------------
def calc_opt_sums(ramp_data, rn_sect, gain_sect, data_masked, mask_2d, xvalues, good_pix): in ols_fit.py
    """
    Calculate the sums needed to determine the slope and intercept (and sigma of
    each) using the optimal weights.  For each good pixel's segment, from the
    initial and final indices and the corresponding number of counts, calculate
    the SNR. From the SNR, calculate the weighting exponent using the formulation
    by Fixsen (Fixsen et al, PASP, 112, 1350). Using this exponent and the gain
    and the readnoise, the weights are calculated from which the sums are
    calculated.
